WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.743
Marius still lived in the old Gorbeau house, but he paid no attention to anyone living there.

2
00:00:22.804 --> 00:00:28.647
The only other tenants left in the building besides himself were the Jondrettes, the miserable family whose rent

3
00:00:28.687 --> 00:00:29.268
he had paid.

4
00:00:29.992 --> 00:00:32.854
It was now February of 1832.

5
00:00:33.755 --> 00:00:36.778
Marius went out for dinner one night as it was growing dark.

6
00:00:37.418 --> 00:00:39.940
He was lost in his own thoughts as he walked along.

7
00:00:40.921 --> 00:00:43.983
Suddenly he felt someone elbow him in the side.

8
00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:47.686
He looked up and saw two girls in rags running past him.

9
00:00:48.807 --> 00:00:52.150
They were breathless, frightened, and obviously in flight.

10
00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:56.093
Apparently they hadn't seen him and had jostled him in passing.

11
00:00:57.134 --> 00:01:01.837
Marius could see their tangled hair, tattered skirts, and bare feet.

12
00:01:02.417 --> 00:01:04.218
They were talking to each other as they ran.

13
00:01:04.238 --> 00:01:07.559
"The cops came," said the taller one.

14
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.701
"I thought they were going to arrest me!"

15
00:01:10.721 --> 00:01:15.943
"I saw them and ran as fast as I could," replied the other girl.

16
00:01:17.524 --> 00:01:19.445
They darted under the trees of the boulevard.

17
00:01:19.905 --> 00:01:24.087
Their clothing made a pale glow in the darkness, which soon faded away.

18
00:01:24.987 --> 00:01:27.068
Marius stopped to watch them for a moment.

19
00:01:27.636 --> 00:01:32.298
He was about to start walking again when he spotted a small gray packet on the ground.

20
00:01:33.138 --> 00:01:34.579
He bent over and picked it up.

21
00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:37.580
It was an envelope containing some papers.

22
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.281
"Those poor girls must have dropped this."

23
00:01:40.982 --> 00:01:43.663
He called after them, but they had already disappeared.

24
00:01:44.483 --> 00:01:48.945
That evening when he was back in his room, he took out the envelope that he had found.

25
00:01:49.785 --> 00:01:53.567
Perhaps if he opened it, he would find an address for one of the girls.

26
00:01:54.187 --> 00:01:56.068
The envelope contained four letters.

27
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.485
These letters were addressed to different people, but in each one, the writer was begging for money.

28
00:02:03.285 --> 00:02:10.391
Two letters were addressed to noblewomen, another to a businessman, and the last to "the generous man" who

29
00:02:10.431 --> 00:02:13.553
attends mass at the Church of Saint Jacques du Haut Pas.

30
00:02:15.035 --> 00:02:22.260
The letters seemed to come from four different individuals: a Spanish cavalry officer; an unfortunate mother of six

31
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.842
children; a poet; and P.

32
00:02:24.922 --> 00:02:26.504
Fabantou, an actor.

33
00:02:27.392 --> 00:02:32.256
But strangely enough, they were all written in the same handwriting on the same kind of coarse yellow

34
00:02:32.316 --> 00:02:36.198
paper. And they all smelled of the same wretched tobacco.

35
00:02:36.759 --> 00:02:41.963
Some effort had been made to change the writing style by varying the length of words and sentences.

36
00:02:42.863 --> 00:02:45.985
But the same spelling errors occurred across all four letters.

37
00:02:46.726 --> 00:02:49.368
There was only one thing that Marius could conclude.

38
00:02:49.388 --> 00:02:53.871
"They must have all been written by the same person," he said to himself.

39
00:02:53.891 --> 00:02:54.912
"But why?

40
00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.401
And how were those girls involved?"

41
00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:00.542
It was too great a puzzle for Marius.

42
00:03:01.263 --> 00:03:05.444
He returned the letters to the envelope, tossed it in a corner, and went to bed.

43
00:03:06.465 --> 00:03:11.367
At seven o'clock the next morning, Marius had just started working when there was a tap at his

44
00:03:11.387 --> 00:03:16.970
door. Marius owned nothing of value, so he hardly ever locked his door.

45
00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:21.972
In fact, when he went out, he left the key in the lock on the outside.

46
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:28.225
The housekeeper had warned him that he would be robbed someday, and indeed someone had stolen an old

47
00:03:28.285 --> 00:03:28.965
pair of boots.

48
00:03:29.666 --> 00:03:32.729
But that loss was not enough to make Marius change his habits.

49
00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.771
There was another gentle rap at the door.

50
00:03:35.791 --> 00:03:39.515
"Come in," said Marius, thinking it was the housekeeper.

51
00:03:39.535 --> 00:03:41.036
"What do you want?"

52
00:03:41.436 --> 00:03:44.859
he asked without raising his eyes from the books and papers on his table.

53
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:53.074
A voice replied, "I beg your pardon, monsieur It was a rasping voice like that of a congested

54
00:03:53.134 --> 00:03:53.714
old man.

55
00:03:54.714 --> 00:03:58.777
Marius turned quickly and saw a girl standing in the half-open door.

56
00:03:59.917 --> 00:04:05.700
She was a pale, thin creature dressed only in a ragged blouse and skirt with a string for

57
00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:06.161
a belt.

58
00:04:07.721 --> 00:04:13.064
Marius stared in astonishment at her dirty shoulders, red hands, and bare feet.

59
00:04:14.165 --> 00:04:19.728
Perhaps she had once been pretty, but now she was missing some teeth and her eyes were dull.

60
00:04:20.672 --> 00:04:21.332
"What do you want?"

61
00:04:21.633 --> 00:04:22.153
he asked.

62
00:04:22.173 --> 00:04:25.835
"Here's a letter for you, Monsieur Marius."

63
00:04:26.796 --> 00:04:30.418
Without waiting for him to invite her in, the girl entered the room.

64
00:04:31.438 --> 00:04:35.060
Large holes in her skirt revealed her long limbs and sharp knees.

65
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:36.481
She was shivering.

66
00:04:37.021 --> 00:04:38.662
Marius began to read the letter.

67
00:04:39.843 --> 00:04:46.907
My dear neighbor: I have learned of your kindness toward me, that you paid my rent last summer.

68
00:04:47.607 --> 00:04:48.848
Bless you, young man.

69
00:04:49.928 --> 00:04:53.870
My daughter will tell you that we have been without a morsel of bread for two days.

70
00:04:54.491 --> 00:04:56.532
There are four of us and my spouse is sick.

71
00:04:57.593 --> 00:05:03.736
I hope that I may rely on your generous heart to lavish upon me some small gift.

72
00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:11.821
Jondrette Marius immediately recognized that this letter came from the same source as the other four.

73
00:05:12.661 --> 00:05:16.764
There was the identical handwriting on the same paper that smelled of tobacco.

74
00:05:17.736 --> 00:05:21.218
It must have been the Jondrette girls whom he encountered the previous night!

75
00:05:22.119 --> 00:05:25.000
Meanwhile the girl was pacing Marius' room.

76
00:05:25.961 --> 00:05:31.664
She moved the chairs; she examined his things on the bureau; she patted his clothes and searched the

77
00:05:31.724 --> 00:05:32.584
corners of the room.

78
00:05:32.604 --> 00:05:35.666
"Oh, you have a mirror!"

79
00:05:36.046 --> 00:05:37.727
she said, preening in front of it.

80
00:05:38.647 --> 00:05:40.589
All the while she hummed to herself.

81
00:05:41.349 --> 00:05:46.592
Nothing was more sorrowful than to see her amusing herself by flitting around the room like a bird.

82
00:05:47.564 --> 00:05:51.766
With the right upbringing, this girl might have been someone sweet and charming.

83
00:05:52.447 --> 00:05:54.968
Marius watched her as she moved toward his table.

84
00:05:54.988 --> 00:05:57.209
"Ah, books!"

85
00:05:58.110 --> 00:05:59.010
Her eyes brightened.

86
00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:01.512
"I can read."

87
00:06:02.192 --> 00:06:04.173
She picked up an open book and read aloud.

88
00:06:04.193 --> 00:06:13.638
"‘General Bauduin received the order to take five battalions to the chateau in the middle of the plain

89
00:06:13.898 --> 00:06:15.019
of Waterloo.'"

90
00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:16.820
She stopped.

91
00:06:17.564 --> 00:06:18.484
"I know Waterloo.

92
00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:20.745
It was a battle against the English.

93
00:06:21.346 --> 00:06:22.286
My father was there."

94
00:06:23.447 --> 00:06:25.627
The girl put down the book and took up a pen.

95
00:06:25.647 --> 00:06:27.108
"I can write too!

96
00:06:27.468 --> 00:06:28.188
Would you like to see?"

97
00:06:29.009 --> 00:06:32.090
She dipped the pen in ink and wrote, "The cops are here."

98
00:06:33.151 --> 00:06:35.592
Throwing down the pen, she said, "Look at this!

99
00:06:36.412 --> 00:06:38.453
There are no mistakes in spelling.

100
00:06:39.153 --> 00:06:41.114
My sister and I received an education.

101
00:06:41.654 --> 00:06:43.695
We haven't always been like this."

102
00:06:44.635 --> 00:06:45.956
Then she gazed at Marius.

103
00:06:46.696 --> 00:06:49.058
"You're a very handsome boy, monsieur."

104
00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:51.480
Marius blushed and turned away from her.

105
00:06:52.781 --> 00:06:55.364
"I have an envelope here that I think belongs to you."

106
00:06:56.064 --> 00:06:58.386
He handed her the letters and she clapped her hands.

107
00:06:58.406 --> 00:07:00.748
"Oh, thank you!

108
00:07:01.549 --> 00:07:04.692
My sister and I had to lie and tell our father that we had delivered these.

109
00:07:05.993 --> 00:07:10.517
Now I'll take this one to the old generous fellow who goes to mass, and perhaps he'll give

110
00:07:10.557 --> 00:07:11.838
us something for our breakfast.

111
00:07:12.779 --> 00:07:14.020
We haven't eaten for days."

112
00:07:15.072 --> 00:07:17.234
That reminded Marius of her father's note.

113
00:07:17.775 --> 00:07:22.999
He felt in his waistcoat pocket and came up with five francs, which he handed to the girl.

114
00:07:23.019 --> 00:07:28.184
"Thank you, monsieur, and now I'm off to find the old man."

115
00:07:29.245 --> 00:07:33.428
On her way out, she spotted a moldy crust of bread in the dust on the bureau.

116
00:07:34.089 --> 00:07:36.071
She pounced on it and ate it.
